Open and close principle means - Open for extension and closed for modification

in general - when a new requirement comes try to add new code instead of changing existing one.

for example
2. O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
Simple definition

Classes should be open for extension but closed for modification.

This sounds complicated, but the idea is simple:

When adding a new feature, try to add new code instead of modifying existing working code.

❌ Bad example

Suppose we have payment processing:

@Service
public class PaymentService {


    public void pay(String type) {


        if (type.equals("CREDIT_CARD")) {
            // credit card payment
        }
        else if (type.equals("PAYPAL")) {
            // PayPal payment
        }
        else if (type.equals("UPI")) {
            // UPI payment
        }
    }
}

Today we have:

Credit Card
PayPal
UPI

Tomorrow:

Google Pay
Stripe
Apple Pay

We have to keep modifying PaymentService.

That's not ideal.

✅ Better approach

Create an interface:

public interface Payment {


    void pay();
}

Then implementations:

@Service
public class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {


    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Credit Card Payment");
    }
}
@Service
public class UpiPayment implements Payment {


    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("UPI Payment");
    }
}

Now if tomorrow we need PayPal:

@Service
public class PaypalPayment implements Payment {


    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("PayPal Payment");
    }
}

We added a new class instead of modifying existing payment classes.

That's OCP.

Think:
Payment
   ↑
   |
--------------------------
|          |             |
Credit     UPI          PayPal
Card

Spring Boot makes this pattern very easy because Spring can inject implementations through dependency injection.